  "query_preview": "..\n\nYesterday's high temperatures once again reached into the mid 60s \nacross most areas, as the 00Z DTX sounding checked in with a 925 mb \ntemp of 11 C. Temperatures should be even warmer today (65 to 70 \ndegrees) as 925 mb temps are advertised to reach near 13 C. Thus, \nthere is an excellent chance Flint ties or breaks its daily record \nhigh for Nov 21. A weaker pressure gradient over eastern areas will \nallow surface flow backed a bit more out of the south-southeast to \nexert some cooler marine influence and thus result in the lower \nvalues.   \n\nAn upper-level wave and cold pool sinking south into Montana today \nwill team up with a strong PV anomaly descending south out of the \nArctic Circle and sliding south along the Manitoba/Ontario border on \nSunday. This will lead to much colder temperatures next week. One \nmore warm day is expected on Sunday, with temperatures likely rising \nback into the low to mid 60s as a cold front and rain showers arrive \nlate in the day into early Sunday evening. With the main low and \nheight falls tracking north of Lower Michigan, indications are that \nthe line of showers along the frontal boundary will be in a \nweakening state as it crosses Southeast Michigan, and QPF amounts \nwill likely be under a tenth of an inch, with some locations \nstruggling to even measure.\n\nAggressive drying arrives by midnight as 700 mb dewpoint depressions \nincrease to 30 C by Monday morning. Nonetheless, a strong upper-\nlevel wave will swing through northern Lower Michigan Monday evening \nand send 850 mb temps into the -9 to -12 C range. There is an \nincreasing chance (supported by the Euro model) that the northern \nThumb region will see light accumulating snow, as there is even \nsynoptic support from the narrow, short-lived forcing along the \nArctic boundary tracking through Monday evening. A 700 mb cold pool \n(-20\u00c2\u00b0C) will track across Lake Huron Monday night, with warming and \nan upper-level ridge quickly building in on Tuesday, putting an end \nto any potential lake-effect activity. The low-level air mass will \nremain chilly as 850 mb temps are progged to hold in the negative \nmid single digits, resulting in maxes holding in the low to mid 40s \nfor the midweek period.",
